      "hint": "Never name a full set of unknown coefficients and never expand a composed or high-degree identity; that always exhausts your budget. Instead recenter: shift to coordinates about an unknown center c and try the smallest symmetric ansatz, e.g. for an identity in two same-degree maps take f=c+a(x-c)^k and g=c-a(x-c)^k, where odd k makes both sides collapse identically. The two supplied values then differ only in the sign of the a-term: add them to isolate c, subtract to isolate a. Verify by that symmetry, not by expanding. Ask whether a requested input is itself the center or a fixed point, making its value immediate. Ugly fractions or divisor-hunting mean the ansatz is wrong, not the arithmetic: switch once, then commit, state your result, and stop re-deriving.",
